WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.574
Picture the coffee-shop payments app at the end of the month.

2
00:00:06.574 --> 00:00:09.813
A report adds up all 2 million customers' balances.

3
00:00:09.813 --> 00:00:11.211
That takes 30 seconds.

4
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:17.022
Meanwhile, 300 times a second, a payment moves money from one balance to another.

5
00:00:17.022 --> 00:00:21.891
No payment creates or destroys money, so the total must come out exact.

6
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:22.738
It does.

7
00:00:22.738 --> 00:00:25.738
And not one payment waited for the report.

8
00:00:25.738 --> 00:00:29.524
Yet the report read rows that were changing under it.

9
00:00:29.524 --> 00:00:29.809
How?

10
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.503
Let's start where most of us start.

11
00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:36.593
The balances sit in a table called wallets, one row per customer.

12
00:00:36.593 --> 00:00:41.691
And we picture each row as one copy, which an update overwrites, like a variable.

13
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:44.368
With one copy, there are two choices.

14
00:00:44.368 --> 00:00:49.435
Choice one: the report locks the table, a mark that says wait until I'm done.

15
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:54.689
The total is right, and about 9,000 transfers queue for up to 30 seconds.

16
00:00:54.689 --> 00:00:57.287
Card readers give up long before that.

17
00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:58.984
Choice two: no lock.

18
00:00:58.984 --> 00:01:03.238
The report reads whatever each row holds when it gets there.

19
00:01:03.238 --> 00:01:04.301
Watch wallet 7.

20
00:01:04.301 --> 00:01:07.279
The report has already counted its 500.00.

21
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:14.520
Halfway through, a transfer moves 250.00 from wallet 7 to wallet 1,800,000.

22
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.339
Then the report reaches wallet 1,800,000.

23
00:01:18.339 --> 00:01:19.830
What goes wrong?

24
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.320
Pause and guess.

25
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.771
That 250.00 is counted twice.

26
00:01:26.771 --> 00:01:33.113
Once in wallet 7, before it left, and once in wallet 1,800,000, after it arrived.

27
00:01:33.113 --> 00:01:36.872
And the next run is wrong by a different amount.

28
00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:37.632
Well...

29
00:01:37.632 --> 00:01:40.218
the report just invented 250.00.

30
00:01:40.218 --> 00:01:42.804
The auditors won't be impressed.

31
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.447
So, wait and be right, or don't wait and be wrong.

32
00:01:46.447 --> 00:01:47.551
Neither is great.

33
00:01:47.551 --> 00:01:51.903
What we want is both: the report sees one moment, and nobody waits.

34
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:55.228
Picture a notebook where nothing is ever rubbed out.

35
00:01:55.228 --> 00:02:01.299
To change a balance, you cross out the old line, write the new balance on a fresh line, and sign both.

36
00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:06.570
Someone who started reading at 18:00 can skip every change made after 18:00.

37
00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:11.408
They read the notebook as it stood when they began, while you keep writing.

38
00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:12.136
Map it.

39
00:02:12.136 --> 00:02:14.416
Each line is a version of the row.

40
00:02:14.416 --> 00:02:17.031
The signatures are transaction numbers.

41
00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:23.939
And the reader's 18:00 is called a snapshot: a note of which transactions had finished when it started.

42
00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:25.577
Now the real thing.

43
00:02:25.577 --> 00:02:36.191
PostgreSQL, a free database program, keeps a table in pages of 8 KB, and each page holds stored row versions, called tuples, in numbered slots.

44
00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:39.254
Every tuple has a header with two numbers.

45
00:02:39.254 --> 00:02:42.638
x-min is the transaction that created this version.

46
00:02:42.638 --> 00:02:46.620
x-max is the one that replaced it, or zero while nobody has.

47
00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:52.672
Wallet 7 sits in slot 1: 500.00, created by transaction 850.

48
00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:56.369
Then transaction 860 moves the 250.00.

49
00:02:56.369 --> 00:02:58.802
It doesn't touch the 500.

50
00:02:58.802 --> 00:03:07.852
It stamps slot 1 with x-max 860, and writes a whole new version in slot 2: 250.00, x-min 860.

51
00:03:07.852 --> 00:03:11.549
A pointer joins the two, oldest first.

52
00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:16.153
The report's snapshot was taken while 860 was still running.

53
00:03:16.153 --> 00:03:18.126
Which version does it read?

54
00:03:18.126 --> 00:03:19.296
Pause and guess.

55
00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:23.788
Slot 1: 500.00.

56
00:03:23.788 --> 00:03:29.558
To its snapshot, 860 hasn't finished, so its stamp doesn't count yet.

57
00:03:29.558 --> 00:03:34.492
A query that starts after 860 commits reads slot 2: 250.00.

58
00:03:34.492 --> 00:03:39.008
Same row, two readers, two answers, and nobody waited.

59
00:03:39.233 --> 00:03:41.648
That's why the report's total is exact.

60
00:03:41.648 --> 00:03:44.559
Every wallet is read as it stood at one moment.

61
00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:56.147
This design is called multi-version concurrency control, MVCC for short: keep several versions of each row, so readers and writers can work at the same time.

62
00:03:56.367 --> 00:04:02.112
MySQL and Oracle's database do it too, and keep their old versions in different places.

63
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.554
So an update isn't an overwrite.

64
00:04:04.554 --> 00:04:06.507
It's a delete plus an insert.

65
00:04:06.507 --> 00:04:10.613
Try it on a small table: three wallets, then update wallet 2.

66
00:04:10.613 --> 00:04:13.239
How many tuples does the page hold now?

67
00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:14.316
Pause and guess.

68
00:04:17.533 --> 00:04:19.687
Four tuples for three rows.

69
00:04:19.687 --> 00:04:26.784
Slot 2 is still there, stamped with x-max 858, and wallet 2's new balance sits in slot 4.

70
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.792
Of course, nothing is free.

71
00:04:28.792 --> 00:04:35.111
Old versions pile up, so a cleanup called VACUUM throws away the ones no reader can see any more.

72
00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:40.813
And one forgotten open transaction can hold that cleanup back for the whole database.

73
00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:43.877
Two writers on the same row still take turns.

74
00:04:43.877 --> 00:04:46.563
Versions free the readers, not the writers.

75
00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:48.690
And here the notebook breaks.

76
00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.276
Its reader goes by the clock: skip anything after 18:00.

77
00:04:52.276 --> 00:04:54.837
A database can't simply go by the clock.

78
00:04:54.837 --> 00:04:58.807
How it picks the right version is the next part of the lesson.

79
00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:03.314
So the moral: an update leaves the old version behind and stamps it.

80
00:05:03.314 --> 00:05:06.776
Each reader picks the one version its snapshot can see.

81
00:05:06.776 --> 00:05:09.357
Keep the old row, and nobody has to wait.

82
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.122
One quick check.

83
00:05:11.122 --> 00:05:14.329
Say 860 rolled back instead of committing.

84
00:05:14.329 --> 00:05:16.315
A query starts afterwards.

85
00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:19.293
Which version of wallet 7 does it read?

86
00:05:19.293 --> 00:05:20.515
Pause and think.

87
00:05:23.733 --> 00:05:25.202
Slot 1: 500.00.

88
00:05:25.202 --> 00:05:32.643
860's stamp only counts if 860 committed, so slot 2 never counts for anyone.
